    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Old Great Bulgaria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Great_Bulgaria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nk, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> tribes, Turkic-speaking pastoralists of the Oghuric branch, who had belonged to the confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> broke up that confederation in the later seventh century, some Bulgars moved west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, where they founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, while others migrated north up the Volga. During the eighth and ninth centuries these groups settled in the forest-steppe around the confluence of the Volga and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kama-river">Kama</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a>, and established their rule over the Finnic and other peoples of the region, among them ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udmurts">Udmurts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mordvins">Mordvins</a>, as well as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burtas">Burtas</a>. The newcomers comprised several clans or tribes, of which the Bulgars proper, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvar">Suvar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esegel">Esegel</a> are named in the sources. The date of 700 used here is only a convenient approximation, since the migration was gradual and the earliest stages are known mainly from archaeology. For two centuries the Volga Bulgars remained tributaries of the Khazar Khaganate, which controlled the lower Volga and the trade route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. Their ruler bore the title <em>yiltawar</em> and the Khazars took tribute from his people, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> paid per household according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ibn Fadlan</a>, and held one of his sons as a hostage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/volga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volga.jpg" alt="Volga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yaroslavl._Volga_River._Cathedral_of_the_Dormition_P5212700_2200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexxx1979, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The wealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> came from its position on the great river route between the forests of the north and the Islamic world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">Furs</a> of sable, marten, squirrel and fox, together with honey, wax, hides and slaves, were brought from the northern peoples whom Arab writers called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisu">Wisu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugra">Yura</a>, and from the Rus, and were exchanged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolghar">Bolghar</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, textiles, glass and other goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid</a> lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Hoards show that huge numbers of Islamic silver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> hands on their way to Rus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> in the ninth and tenth centuries. Bulgar rulers struck their own imitation dirhams from the early tenth century. The economy also rested on agriculture: the Bulgars grew wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and barley, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and horses, and had largely settled down by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ibn Fadlan</a>’s visit in 922. Towns such as Bolghar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvar">Suvar</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilyar">Bilyar</a> grew into large craft centres, known for leatherwork, metalwork and pottery, with brick mosques, baths and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread among the ruling elite and townspeople, apparently following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a>, and Muslim scholars, merchants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> connected the region with Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Much of the rural and forest population, however, kept older beliefs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kalka-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kalka-river.jpg" alt="Battle of the Kalka River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kalka-river">Battle of the Kalka River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MongolCavalrymen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sayf al-Vâhidî. Hérât. Afghanistan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The military strength of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> rested on mounted warriors drawn from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> clans, supported by levies from subject peoples and by the defences of its towns, which were surrounded by earth ramparts, ditches and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> walls. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilyar">Bilyar</a>, the chief city of the later period, was ringed by several lines of ramparts and was among the largest fortified settlements of medieval eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In the tenth century the main threats came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and the Rus. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Russian Primary Chronicle</a> records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir of Kiev</a> campaigned against the Bulgars in 985 and then made peace with them. From the late eleventh century the principal enemy became the rising principality of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> on the upper Volga, with which the Bulgars competed for control of the river trade and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnic-peoples">Finnic peoples</a> between them. The Bulgars besieged Suzdal in 1107, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> led a major campaign against them in 1164, taking the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryakhimov">Bryakhimov</a>. Raids and counter-raids continued: the Bulgars seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-ustyug">Ustyug</a> around 1218, and in 1220 a Vladimir army sacked the Bulgar town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oshel">Oshel</a>. In 1221 Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-ii-of-vladimir">Yuri II</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> as a forward base against them. In 1223 the Bulgars ambushed and defeated part of the Mongol army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-samara-bend">Samara Bend</a> as it returned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kalka-river">Battle of the Kalka River</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Sviatoslav I of Kiev" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sviatoslav_by_Eugene_Lanceray_1886.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yevgeny Lansere, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was a monarchy built on a confederation of clans. The supreme ruler, called <em>yiltawar</em> in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ibn Fadlan</a> and later <em>emir</em> or king, stood above subordinate chiefs, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvar">Suvar</a> had their own ruler in the tenth century, who sometimes resisted the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolghar">Bolghar</a>. The conversion of the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was the defining act of state-building. In 921 the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alm-s">Almish</a>, son of Shilki, asked the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Muqtadir for religious teachers, builders of a mosque and a fortress, and money, hoping for support against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>. The embassy, whose secretary Ibn Fadlan left a vivid account, arrived in May 922. Almish publicly adopted Islam, had the Friday sermon read in the caliph’s name and took the name Jafar ibn Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>. His son Mikail and later rulers issued coins bearing their names and that of the caliph, signs of a ruler claiming legitimacy in the wider Islamic world. Little is known in detail about the administration, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> collected tribute from subject peoples and tolls on trade, kept a court with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> and religious officials, and conducted diplomacy with Rus princes and Muslim rulers. The decline of Khazaria after the campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a> in 965 freed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> from tribute and allowed full independence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle.jpg" alt="Primary Chronicle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:14_2_List_of_Radzivill_Chron.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">from the Middle Ages, unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was the first state of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as its official religion, and for more than three centuries it was the northernmost outpost of the Islamic world, making the middle Volga a frontier of Muslim culture deep in the forest zone. Its merchants connected the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> that flowed through its markets helped stimulate commerce among the Rus and in the Baltic. Muslim travellers left important descriptions of it: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ibn Fadlan</a>’s account of his journey, including his description of a Rus funeral on the Volga, is one of the main sources for the early history of eastern Europe, and the Andalusian traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hamid-al-gharnati">Abu Hamid al-Gharnati</a> visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolghar">Bolghar</a> in the twelfth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Russian Primary Chronicle</a> claims that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> Muslims tried to win <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir of Kiev</a> for Islam before his conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, a story probably shaped by later tradition. After the Mongol conquest the region remained a centre of Islamic culture under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and later the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>. Both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-tatars">Volga Tatars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuvash-people">Chuvash</a> regard the Bulgars as ancestors, and the Chuvash language is the only living descendant of the Oghuric branch of Turkic that the Bulgars spoke. The ruins of Bolghar became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2014.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batu-khan.jpg" alt="Batu Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batu_Khan_on_the_Throne_by_Rashid_al-Din_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rashid al-Din Hamadani, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. After its success at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-samara-bend">Samara Bend</a> in 1223, the state faced renewed pressure as Mongol forces campaigned on the lower Volga and in the steppes from about 1229, defeating its neighbours and harrying its frontier outposts. The decision of the great khan Ögedei to launch a western campaign under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> sealed its fate. In the autumn of 1236 a large Mongol army invaded the country, stormed and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilyar">Bilyar</a> and other towns, and killed or enslaved much of the population; Russian chronicles describe the slaughter and flight of refugees to the Russian principalities. Resistance continued for a short time, and local leaders rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> again in the following years before the revolts were crushed. The country was then incorporated into the Ulus of Jochi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolghar">Bolghar</a> recovered and became one of the Horde’s important cities, where coins were minted and where Batu appears to have kept a seat before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> was built. In the fourteenth century the town flourished again, with large stone mosques and a mausoleum, before the troubles of the Horde and the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and Russian raiders ruined it. The centre of the region later shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, capital of the Khanate of Kazan from the fifteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> made the middle Volga a meeting point of the northern forests and the Islamic world, becoming the first state of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 922 and growing rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed it in 1236, leaving a legacy claimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuvash-people">Chuvash</a>.</em></p>

